From: Grische <opensource@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 20:21:11 +0200
Subject: wifi: ath10k: clear the HTT tx queue state of a removed peer

In push-pull mode the firmware fetches a host queue whenever its peer
bit is set in htt_q_state.map. The only writer of a peer's count and map
bit is __ath10k_htt_tx_txq_recalc(), which runs for a live queue, so a
station or vif that goes away with frames queued leaves its entries set.
The firmware does not reuse peer ids while it runs, so the stale entry
stays set for the rest of the boot.

This causes the firmware to see queued frames for a peer that no longer
exists. If it then fetches that peer, the host finds no queue for it
and skips the record with the log
  "failed to lookup txq for peer_id N tid T"

On a big-endian host with the byte-swapped map, the firmware reads such
a stale bit as the bit of another peer.

Which clients were starved depends on which stations had left before,
so the stall appeared to move randomly between clients.

Zero the count and clear the map bit in every TID of the peer when the
station or the vif is removed, and publish the change with a new
sequence number.

Tested-on: QCA9984 hw1.0 PCI 10.4-3.9.0.2-00157

Fixes: c1a43d9720d8 ("ath10k: implement updating shared htt txq state")
Assisted-by: LLM sparse
Signed-off-by: Grische <opensource@grische.xyz>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt.h    |  1 +
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c    |  3 +++
 3 files changed, 29 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt.h
@@ -2452,6 +2452,7 @@ void ath10k_htt_tx_txq_update(struct iee
 void ath10k_htt_tx_txq_recalc(struct ieee80211_hw *hw,
 			      struct ieee80211_txq *txq);
 void ath10k_htt_tx_txq_sync(struct ath10k *ar);
+void ath10k_htt_tx_txq_clear_peer(struct ath10k *ar, u16 peer_id);
 void ath10k_htt_tx_dec_pending(struct ath10k_htt *htt);
 int ath10k_htt_tx_inc_pending(struct ath10k_htt *htt);
 void ath10k_htt_tx_mgmt_dec_pending(struct ath10k_htt *htt);
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c
@@ -141,6 +141,31 @@ void ath10k_htt_tx_txq_update(struct iee
 	spin_unlock_bh(&ar->htt.tx_lock);
 }
 
+void ath10k_htt_tx_txq_clear_peer(struct ath10k *ar, u16 peer_id)
+{
+	__le32 bit = cpu_to_le32(BIT(peer_id % 32));
+	int idx = peer_id / 32;
+	u8 tid;
+
+	spin_lock_bh(&ar->htt.tx_lock);
+
+	if (!ar->htt.tx_q_state.enabled ||
+	    peer_id >= ar->htt.tx_q_state.num_peers)
+		goto unlock;
+
+	for (tid = 0; tid < ar->htt.tx_q_state.num_tids; tid++) {
+		ar->htt.tx_q_state.vaddr->count[tid][peer_id] = 0;
+		ar->htt.tx_q_state.vaddr->map[tid][idx] &= ~bit;
+	}
+
+	ath10k_dbg(ar, ATH10K_DBG_HTT, "htt tx txq state clear peer_id %u\n",
+		   peer_id);
+
+	__ath10k_htt_tx_txq_sync(ar);
+unlock:
+	spin_unlock_bh(&ar->htt.tx_lock);
+}
+
 void ath10k_htt_tx_dec_pending(struct ath10k_htt *htt)
 {
 	lockdep_assert_held(&htt->tx_lock);
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c
@@ -6113,6 +6113,7 @@ static void ath10k_remove_interface(stru
 	spin_unlock_bh(&ar->htt.tx_lock);
 
 	ath10k_mac_txq_unref(ar, vif->txq);
+	ath10k_htt_tx_txq_clear_peer(ar, arvif->peer_id);
 
 out:
 	mutex_unlock(&ar->conf_mutex);
@@ -7761,6 +7762,8 @@ static int ath10k_sta_state(struct ieee8
 		for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(sta->txq); i++)
 			ath10k_mac_txq_unref(ar, sta->txq[i]);
 
+		ath10k_htt_tx_txq_clear_peer(ar, arsta->peer_id);
+
 		if (!sta->tdls)
 			goto exit;
 
